1 OCCU Sogneprest 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Nils Joachim Christian Vibe Stockfleth''', Sogneprest 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils was a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway Norwegian] cleric who was instrumental in the first development of the written form of the Northern [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sami_languages Sami language]. Stockfleth compiled a
2 CONC  Norwegian-Sami dictionary, wrote a book on Sami grammar and translated a portion of the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bible Bible] into the Sami language.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born to Dean [http://www.geni.com/people/Nils-Stockfleth/6000000013391983806 Niels Stockfleth] (1756–1794) and his wife [http://www.geni.com/people/Anne-Wibe/6000000013392442264 Anne Johanne Vibe] (1753–1805), he was a student in 
2 CONC [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copenhagen Copenhagen] from 1803 to 1804, when he was hired as an undersecretary in the Danish [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chancery_%28medieval_office%29 Chancery] office (Danske Kancelli). He atten
2 CONC ded lectures on law, and for a time he studied carpentry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1808 he was commissioned as a lieutenant in the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_Army Danish Army] and took part in the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sehested Battle of Sehested] ([http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sch
2 CONC leswig-Holstein Schleswig-Holstein]) during the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleonic_Wars Napoleonic Wars]. After the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denmark%E2%80%93Norway Denmark-Norway] union ended in 1814, Stockfleth joined 
2 CONC the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norwegian_Army Norwegian Army] as an officer posted to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdres Valdres]. He resigned from the army in 1823 to study theology, graduating in 1824. In March of the foll
2 CONC owing year he became pastor of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vads%C3%B8 Vadsø]. His first journey to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapland_%28region%29 Lappland] would begin aboard the three-masted merchant vessel Najaden which wa
2 CONC s on it’s way to Vadsø. The ship left [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helsingborg Helsingborg] on the 2nd of May 1825, with Nils, his brother John and the young [http://www.geni.com/people/Hans-Esbensen/6000000008418635582 Hans Pett
2 CONC er Esbensen], the son of the ship owner and merchant [http://www.geni.com/people/Arent-Esbensen/6000000008617979610 Arent Nicolai Esbensen]. An account of the journey “[http://www.altabibliotek.net/finnmark/Stockfleth/Dagbog.php?k
2 CONC ap=1825&pt=1 Min første Reise til Finmarken]”, was written in his 1860 book “[http://www.altabibliotek.net/finnmark/Stockfleth/Dagbog.php?kap=Portrett Dagbog over mine Missionsreiser i Finmarken]”. He was later transferred to [htt
2 CONC p://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebesby Lebesby] in 1828 so that he could more easily meet with the nomadic Sami. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1836 Stockfleth taught Sami languages and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnish_language Finnish] in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oslo Christiania] (now the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Oslo University of Oslo
2 CONC ]). In 1838 he travelled to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finland Finland] and gained the support of Finnish philologists [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_Renvall Gustaf Renvall] (1781–1841), Reinhold von Becker (1788–1858), A
2 CONC ndreas Sjögren (1794–1855) and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elias_L%C3%B6nnrot Elias Lönnrot] (1802–1884).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1839 he ended his pastoral duties to devote himself fully to understanding Sami culture, travelling several times to Sami and Finnish settlements in both Norway and [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweden Sweden]. Influenced by En
2 CONC lightenment thinkers like [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Gottfried_Herder Johann Gottfried Herder], he worked assiduously for the betterment of the Sami people, especially in the literary field. Stockfleth and the Danish pol
2 CONC yglot and philologist [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasmus_Christian_Rask Rasmus Christian Rask] cooperated to develop a means of accurately recording a written form of the Sami language so that it could be used as a medium for th
2 CONC e publication of religious books.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a time when powerful people, both in government and in the press, believed that the Sami people should be forced to attend schools with Norwegian being the sole language of instruction and that Sami-language teaching would dela
2 CONC y efforts to modernize and assimilate the Sami people, Stockfleth succeeded in publishing several Sami readers and a Sami grammar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851 Stockfleth travelled for the last time to the Finnmark region of Norway. The [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lutheran_Church_in_Norway Lutheran] bishop of Oslo immediately encouraged him to go to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wik
2 CONC i/Kautokeino Kautokeino] in the hope that Stockfleth, who knew the Sami culture, was fluent in Sami and was respected among the Sami because of his books, would be able to reconcile a group of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laestad
2 CONC ianism Laestadian] Sami schismatics with the official Lutheran state church. He did meet many of the Sami at one of their religious meetings, but they were in the grip of an experiential ecstasy which was quite foreign to the lear
2 CONC ned theologian. At one point he lost his temper and began to beat the ecstatic participants with his hands and with a stick, but to no avail. Although he found the uproar of this first meeting frightening, some of the Sami people 
2 CONC did continue to meet with him afterwards, but little came of their talks and Stockfleth travelled away from Finnmark a few months before the [http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kautokeinooppr%C3%B8ret Kautokeino Uprising]. In 1853 he wa
2 CONC s awarded a state pension and moved to [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandefjord Sandefjord].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Selected works===
2 CONT
2 CONT *     1840: Grammatik i det lappiske Sprog, saaledes som det tales i norsk-Finmarken. (Grammar of Sami, Norwegian Finnmark dialect). Christiania: Grøndahl.
2 CONT *     1840: Det nye testamentet (New Testament in Sami)
2 CONT *     1848: Bidrag til kundskab om finnerne i kongeriget Norge
2 CONT *     1848: Bidrag til kundskab om qvænerne i kongeriget Norge
2 CONT *     1852: Norsk-lappisk ordbog (Norwegian-Sami dictionary)
2 CONT *     1860: [http://www.altabibliotek.net/finnmark/Stockfleth/Dagbog.php?kap=Portrett Dagbog over mine missionsreiser i Finmarken] ("Journal of my mission trips to Finnmark")
2 CONT *     1896: Johannes Nilson Skaar, Posthumous publication of Nils Vibe Stockfleth's Autobiography (to 1825) and Letters (1825–1854).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Honours===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Street in Oslo: [http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stockfleths_gate_%28Oslo%29 Stockfleths gate]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Street i Sandefjord: [https://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stockfleths_gate_(Sandefjord%29 Stockfleths gate]
2 CONT
