1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Trondhjem as a son of barrister Arthur Knagenhjelm (1866–1938) and Barbara Ihlen (1870–1953).[1] On the maternal side he was a grandson of Jacob Thurmann Ihlen and Belgian citizen Ambrosine Pauline Rouquet.[2] On th
2 CONC e paternal side he was a nephew of Lord Chamberlain Jacob Roll Knagenhjelm. The Knagenhjelm family had resided in Sogndal in recent times, and had been ennobled in 1721, then under the name Knagenhielm.[3] Through his grandmother 
2 CONC Selma Angelique Lousie Roll he was a great-grandson of mayor Jacob Roll and grandnephew of Oluf Nicolai Roll, Karl Jacob Roll and Ferdinand Nicolai Roll.[4] In June 1946 in Stockholm he married Ragnhild Sverdrup Fearnley, born 191
2 CONC 6.[5] She was a daughter of Ragnhild Sverdrup,[1] who was a second cousin of Otto Sverdrup.[6][7] [edit]Career
2 CONT
2 CONT He finished his secondary education in 1916, and graduated with the cand.jur. degree in 1922. He was a deputy judge from 1922 to 1924, then a junior solicitor from 1925 to 1928. He was hired in the Ministry of Finance in 1930, and
2 CONC  advanced to assistant secretary in 1934. In 1940 he was hired in the Ministry of Provisioning. He became deputy under-secretary of state in the Ministry of Local Government in 1945, and served as the highest-ranking civil servant
2 CONC  in the ministry, permanent under-secretary of state, from 1966 to 1968.[1] He chaired Salomons Skofabrik and was a supervisory council member of Hafslund and Andresens Bank.[1] He died in May 1987 and was buried in Vestre gravlun
2 CONC d.[8] [edit]
