1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bokbindergården (Oslo)
2 CONT "Bokbindergården (også kalt Håndverkergården) var en bygning i Tollbugata 14 i Oslo fra tidlig moderne tid. Den ble i 1915 flyttet til Norsk folkemuseum for bevaring for ettertiden og representerer i dag det eneste bevarte eksempe
2 CONC let på en bygård i utmurt bindingsverk fra Christiania på 1600- og 1700-tallet. "
2 CONT
2 CONT "Det var den lange eierrekken av bokbindere som gjorde at Folkemuseet kalte den bokbindergården. I 1814 overtok konditor Jacob Caspary gården. Caspary-brødrene var fra en håndverkerfamilie som innvandret fra Sveits. "
2 CONT
2 CONT Eier: 1814- Konditor Jacob Caspary
2 CONT
2 CONT English:
2 CONT Bokbindergården (Oslo)
2 CONT "Bokbindergården (also called Håndverkergården) was a building in Tollbugata 14 in Oslo from the early modern period. In 1915 it was moved to the Norwegian Folk Museum for preservation for posterity and today represents the only p
2 CONC reserved example of an apartment building in brick half-timbering from Christiania in 1600 - and the 18th century."
2 CONT
2 CONT "It was the long ownership line of bookbinders that made the Folkemuseum call it the bookbinder farm. In 1814, confectioner Jacob Caspary took over the farm. The Caspary brothers were from a family of craftsmen who immigrated from
2 CONC  Switzerland."
2 CONT
2 CONT Owner: 1814- Pastry chef Jacob Caspary
2 CONT -
2 CONT ===1862 Jacob Casparys enke===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://lokalhistoriewiki.no/index.php?title=Bokbinderg%C3%A5rden_(...
