1 NAME Mojzesz / Moritz Maurycy (Moryczko) /Horowitz/ JD
2 GIVN Mojzesz / Moritz Maurycy (Moryczko)
2 SURN Horowitz
2 NSFX JD
2 _MARNM Horowitz / Borowicz
1 NAME משה מוריס /הורוביץ/ JD
2 GIVN משה מוריס
2 SURN הורוביץ
2 NSFX JD
2 _MARNM בורוביץ
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Jewish Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Krakow
3 STAE Lesser Poland Voivodeship
3 CTRY Poland
1 DEAT
2 ADDR
3 CITY Katowice
3 STAE Silesian Voivodeship
3 CTRY Poland
1 GRAD
2 PLAC Jagiellonian University 
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 JUN 1872
2 ADDR
3 CITY Krakow
3 STAE Lesser Poland Voivodeship
3 CTRY Poland
1 OCCU Lawyer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HOROWITZ Maurice, Dr. 08/04/1919 lawyer, Wiieliczka (http://kehilalinks.jewishgen.org/krakow/kra_bnei_brith.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT My father was called Mojzesz-Maurycy Horowitz and was a lawyer by profession. He graduated from Jagiellonian University [1] and all his life, since he settled [there] in 1904 after marrying my mother [on 24th December 1904], he wa
2 CONC s an attorney-at-law in Wieliczka. He ran a private practice. He also had other interests besides law, namely he loved gardening. He took care of that, people were hired for weeding. There was also a caretaker, an ex-miner who liv
2 CONC ed in a cottage belonging to the garden.
2 CONT
2 CONT My dad was an elected vice-mayor of Wieliczka. The mayor was a political nominee, and the vice-mayor was elected by the town council, which was also elected. My father was active on the town council for many years. He was in charg
2 CONC e of the public utilities: sidewalks, sewage system, waterworks, power system, and so on. Wieliczka had those things before Cracow did. My father occupied various important posts in the town. Also among the Jews. For instance, he 
2 CONC ran the health fund for many years. Before the war, each town had its health fund and together they formed a kind of cooperative. There were two health funds in Wieliczka, one for the salt miners, the other for all the other publi
2 CONC c and private factories running a health insurance plan. In the latter one there was a board, and my father served as the president of that board for fifteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father wasn't a PPS [2] member before the war, he sympathized with it, but never joined, I suppose. In any case, the PPS held him in high esteem and he was elected [vice-mayor] surely by the PPS's votes. After the war he immedi
2 CONC ately joined and, as a reward, they transferred him to the PPR [3].
2 CONT
2 CONT My dad wasn't religious but he went to synagogue on every anniversary of his father's death. I never went to synagogue; only once I did go, as an amateur, so to say, to the 'Tempel' in Cracow, a synagogue built, I suppose, by the 
2 CONC B'nai B'rith [4]. My father was strongly attached to Jewishness. He read in Hebrew. We buried him at the Jewish cemetery in Cracow, because that was what he wanted. Our whole family was Jewish, we were all Jews. There were also co
2 CONC nverts in Wieliczka, the Molkners. Molkner was married to a Catholic woman but he met with his relatives regularly, whereas there were also those who preferred to pretend they had never been Jews. Those we ignored.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.ics.uci.edu/~dan/genealogy/Krakow/Families/Gruner.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Mojzesz HOROWITZ          Jun 27, 1872 Wieliczka
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT DURING HOLOCAUST IN: In hiding Wieliczka (today Poland) Ghetto LIBERATED: In hiding in Warsaw
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ics.uci.edu/~dan/genealogy/Krakow/Families/Grunwald.html
