1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  Ravndal, Christian Magelssen (1899-1984) — also known as Christian M. Ravndal — of Iowa. Born in Beirut, Syria (now Lebanon) of American parents, January 6, 1899. Served in the U.S. Army during World War I; Foreig
2 CONC n Service officer; U.S. Vice Consul in Vienna, 1922-24; U.S. Consul in Frankfort, 1924-26; Cologne, 1928-29; Toronto, 1929-30; Vancouver, 1935-37; Buenos Aires, 1938; Stockholm, 1943-47; U.S. Ambassador to Uruguay, 1949-51; Ecuado
2 CONC r, 1956-60; Czechoslovakia, 1960-61; U.S. Minister to Hungary, 1951-56. Died in 1984 (age about 85 years). Burial location unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Magelssen Ravndal (January 6, 1899 Beirut–October 18, 1984 Vienna, Austria) was an American Career Foreign Service Officer FSO who served as the 2nd Director General of the Foreign Service from May 1, 1947, until June 23
2 CONC , 1949, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary to Uruguay (1949–1951), Hungary (1951–1956), Ecuador (1956–1960), and Czechoslovakia (1960–1961), among many other postings during a prolific career spanning 40+ years.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_M._Ravndal Wikipedia]
